n-absolute-advantage-economy">https://yashenghuang.substack.com/p/china-as-an-absolute-advantage-economy</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/06/wednesday-assorted-links-559.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=wednesday-assorted-links-559">Wednesday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://yashenghuang.substack.com/p/china-as-an-absolute-advantage-economy</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://yashenghuang.substack.com/p/china-as-an-absolute-advantage-economy</guid><pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 16:02:51 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Greenspan and Keynes crossed paths in 1944]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://jf.x.com/images/media-preview/2069226969561002189"></p><p>Jon Hartley (@Jon_Hartley_) on X</p><p>Fun Fact: Alan Greenspan &amp; John Maynard Keynes crossed paths. According to Greenspan, in 1944, he stayed at the NH Mt. Washington Hotel during the Bretton Woods Conference (UN Monetary &amp; Fin Conference) traveling as a Henry Jerome Orchestra jazz clarinet player only 1 floor apart</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/Jon_Hartley_/status/2069226969561002189?s=20">https://x.com/Jon_Hartley_/status/2069226969561002189?s=20</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/06/wednesday-assorted-links-559.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=wednesday-assorted-links-559">Wednesday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/Jon_Hartley_/status/2069226969561002189?s=20</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/Jon_Hartley_/status/2069226969561002189?s=20</guid><pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 16:02:51 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Robert Shiller opposes AI negativity]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/22/opinion/ai-doom-jobs-economy.html">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/22/opinion/ai-doom-jobs-economy.html</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/06/wednesday-assorted-links-559.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=wednesday-assorted-links-559">Wednesday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/22/opinion/ai-doom-jobs-economy.html</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/22/opinion/ai-doom-jobs-economy.html</guid><pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 16:02:51 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[The opportunity cost of Trae is really not that large]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2040964334688636929/lrK58DS1_200x200.jpg"></p><p>The Wiz Bob (@The_DC_Bob) on X</p><p>The only rationale for calling the Trae Young extension “bad” is one simple question Does this extension prevent us from extending our young talent in the future? If the answer is no, which it probably is, the dollar figure doesn’t really matter. We’re not paying anybody.</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/The_DC_Bob/status/2069137787912139042">https://x.com/The_DC_Bob/status/2069137787912139042</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/06/wednesday-assorted-links-559.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=wednesday-assorted-links-559">Wednesday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/The_DC_Bob/status/2069137787912139042</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/The_DC_Bob/status/2069137787912139042</guid><pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 16:02:51 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[opportunity cost]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1295802077306855429/MQntnKvN_200x200.jpg"></p><p>Brett Siegel (@BrettSiegelNBA) on X</p><p>It’s not at all. The Wizards have no cap concerns until the end of this contract, and they had to pay someone. Might as well lock in Trae as your PG moving forward and assure him you have full confidence in him to lead the team. I love the deal for both sides &amp; happy for Trae.</p>